Dear Admissions Committee,

I am writing to express my strong motivation to pursue the Master of Arts in Computer Science and Engineering at İstanbul Sabahattin Zaim University. This program represents an alignment between where I am as an engineer and where I need to go as a researcher.

I come to this application with five years of professional experience building production systems. As co-founder and technical lead at Cyborgtech Creative Agency, I managed the development division, shipping client applications and learning how real-world constraints — timeline, budget, user expectations — shape technical decisions. I have also worked as a model tester and QA specialist at Pygmalion.chat, where I evaluate large language models before deployment, a role that taught me to think systematically about quality, reliability, and the gap between what looks good on paper and what actually works in production.

Outside of employment, I have pursued several substantial technical projects. I designed Atlas ERP, an open-source enterprise resource planning system benchmarked against SAP S/4HANA, which forced me to think deeply about data modeling, system architecture, and the trade-offs between flexibility and performance. I lead the technical development of Third Eye Worldwide's platform — a voice-first social network designed with blind users as the default user, not an afterthought. These projects have shown me what I do well: understanding complex domains, designing systems that serve real constraints, and shipping code that actually works. They have also shown me what I lack: formal research training, exposure to the theoretical foundations that separate ad-hoc engineering from principled systems design, and the discipline of a research group.

My undergraduate degree in Computer Science (3.68/4.0 CGPA) gave me the fundamentals. My professional work gave me practice. What I need now is the research perspective — the ability to ask not just "how do I build this?" but "what makes this design correct?" and "how do I verify it?" That is what draws me to your program.

İstanbul Sabahattin Zaim University's MA in Computer Science and Engineering sits at exactly the right level for me. Your program's focus on systems thinking, coupled with its proximity to Istanbul's technology ecosystem and your faculty's research profiles in distributed systems and software architecture, makes this the place where I can ground my self-taught, project-driven experience in formal theory and research methodology. Your program is taught in English, which eliminates a logistical barrier that would otherwise divide my attention. Turkey's welcoming environment for international students, its cultural and geographic proximity to Afghanistan, and its reasonable cost of living mean I can commit fully to my studies without the constant strain of managing visa complexity or financial pressure that comes with studying in Western Europe or North America.

My longer-term goal is a PhD in software engineering or systems research. I see this master's degree as the critical stepping stone — the place where I transition from being a self-taught engineer who reads papers in my spare time to being someone embedded in a research community, accountable to faculty mentors, and trained in how real research problems are scoped, investigated, and validated.

I am ready for this transition. I have the technical foundation, the professional maturity, and the intellectual discipline to contribute meaningfully to your program. I am committed to bringing the same rigor and self-direction that have driven my professional work into the research environment your program provides.

I would welcome the opportunity to study at İstanbul Sabahattin Zaim University.

Sincerely,
Taha Mahmoodi
